{"name":"Scallop Shell","hex":"#fbd8c9","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"scallop shell","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any member of certain species of genus Rheumaptera of moths.","A pilgrim's shell."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/scallop_shell"}],"entries":[{"title":"Scallop","blocks":[{"text":"Scallop is a common name that encompasses various species of marine bivalve molluscs in the taxonomic family Pectinidae, the scallops. However, this common name is also sometimes applied to species in other closely related families within the superfamily Pectinoidea, which also includes the thorny oysters."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/09/Argopecten_irradians.jpg/330px-Argopecten_irradians.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Scallop"},{"title":"Rheumaptera undulata","blocks":[{"text":"Rheumaptera undulata or Hydria undulata, the scallop shell, is a moth of the family Geometridae. It was first described by Carl Linnaeus in his 1758 10th edition of Systema Naturae. It is found in most of the Palearctic realm and North America."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/50/Hydria_undulata.jpg/330px-Hydria_undulata.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rheumaptera_undulata"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a8/Opened_scallop_shell.jpg/500px-Opened_scallop_shell.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a8/Opened_scallop_shell.jpg/500px-Opened_scallop_shell.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A live opened scallop showing the internal anatomy: The pale orange circular part is the adductor muscle; the darker orange curved part is the \"coral\", a culinary term for the ovary or roe.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AOpened_scallop_shell.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/74/Fast_and_slow_adductor_muscles_of_the_Yesso_scallop%2C_Patinopecten_yessoensis.webp?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 1x","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/74/Fast_and_slow_adductor_muscles_of_the_Yesso_scallop%2C_Patinopecten_yessoensis.webp?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","alt":"Adductor muscles of a scallop. a: photo in a live scallop. b: after removal of organs and tissues.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AFast_and_slow_adductor_muscles_of_the_Yesso_scallop%2C_Patinopecten_yessoensis.webp"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/Scallop_eyes2.jpg/500px-Scallop_eyes2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/Scallop_eyes2.jpg/1280px-Scallop_eyes2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/Scallop_eyes2.jpg/500px-Scallop_eyes2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AScallop_eyes2.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/2f/Argopecten_irradians_eye.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 1x","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/2f/Argopecten_irradians_eye.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AArgopecten_irradians_eye.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9c/Purcell%E2%80%99s_swimming_scallop_with_reciprocal_motion.png/500px-Purcell%E2%80%99s_s